{"conjecture": "Software rot arises because each locally-correct change patches the mismatch between an evolving domain model and a frozen code abstraction by adding an adapter or special-case layer rather than refactoring the core. Over time, these accreted layers form a palimpsest where the actual domain semantics can only be reconstructed by mentally composing all layers in reverse, making any further change require understanding an unbounded historical context that no single developer holds. The system becomes hard to change not because any individual layer is wrong, but because the *compositional semantics* of stacked abstraction layers diverge from the domain model in a way that is invisible to local inspection and only manifests as a global complexity barrier."}
